In C programming, you can terminate a program using various methods. Here are some common ways to terminate a C program:

1. Normal termination: A program can be terminated normally by reaching the end of the `main()` function. When the last statement in the `main()` function is executed, the program will terminate and return an exit status to the operating system.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    printf("Program completed successfully.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

2. Returning from a function: You can also terminate a program by returning from a function other than `main()`. However, this is not a recommended way to terminate a program as it may leave the `main()` function in an undefined state.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void terminate() {
    printf("Terminating program.\n");
    exit(0);
}

int main() {
    // Your code here
    terminate();
    return 0; // This statement will not be executed
}
```

3. Using `exit()` function: The `exit()` function is a standard library function that can be used to terminate a program immediately. It takes an integer argument that specifies the exit status.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    if (some_condition) {
        printf("Terminating program.\n");
        exit(1); // Exit status 1 indicates an error
    }
    // This statement will not be executed
}
```

4. Using `abort()` function: The `abort()` function is a standard library function that is used to cause an abrupt termination of the program. It does not return to the calling function or the main function.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    if (some_condition) {
        printf("Terminating program.\n");
        abort(); // Abort the program
    }
    // This statement will not be executed
}
```

